Q30. Generic Box Class
Write a Java program that defines a generic class named Box<T>, capable
of storing a single value of any data type and returning it later.
Demonstrate the flexibility of this generic class by creating separate
Box instances that store an Integer, a String, and a Double
respectively, and display the value retrieved from each.
Concepts: Generics, generic class.

CODE:
class Box<T> {

    private T value;

    public Box() {
    }

    public Box(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public void setValue(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public T getValue() {
        return value;
    }

    public void displayValue() {
        System.out.println("Stored Value : " + value);
    }
}

public class GenericBox {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Box<Integer> integerBox = new Box<>();
        integerBox.setValue(100);

        Box<String> stringBox = new Box<>();
        stringBox.setValue("Java Generics");

        Box<Double> doubleBox = new Box<>();
        doubleBox.setValue(99.99);

        System.out.println("Integer Box");
        integerBox.displayValue();

        System.out.println();

        System.out.println("String Box");
        stringBox.displayValue();

        System.out.println();

        System.out.println("Double Box");
        doubleBox.displayValue();

        System.out.println();

        System.out.println("Retrieved Integer : " + integerBox.getValue());
        System.out.println("Retrieved String  : " + stringBox.getValue());
        System.out.println("Retrieved Double  : " + doubleBox.getValue());
    }
}

